#X obj 60 120 eps.i~ 1;
#X text 190 120 <- click it: the editor, f 64;
#X obj 60 160 dac~;
#X obj 60 206 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty eps.i~ 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 234 eps.i~ is the whole instrument in one object: eps~ \, the editor \, the controller layer \, the Launchkey pads and display \, MIDI in and out. Put it in a patch \, put your Ensoniq instruments into a folder eps beside the patch \, connect the two left outlets to dac~ and play. Click it to open the editor \, see Editor. \; Argument 1 is the MIDI channel (0 or none = 1) \, argument 2 the channel of the pads (0 or none = 10 \, the Drums Channel). Several eps.i~ do not get in each other's way \, each has its own editor and plays its own instrument. \; eps~ is the bare engine inside \, for patches of your own: inlet 2 takes the velocity of a note sent as a float \, inlet 3 raw MIDI bytes from midiin \, and the floats of outlet 3 (MIDI thru) go to midiout. eps~ alone takes the library beside the external \, or the folder given with library. Open eps.i~ to see the wiring., f 64;
#X obj 60 504 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Editor 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 532 The editor is the window of eps.i~. Every change shows up in it \, from the controller \, an instrument change or messages. refresh asks for all values again. \; Rows of 8 below the PATCH band \, titled as in Controls: row k is page k of the controller layer. Each cell: name and label \, a slider and a number box with the value as from a pot (0 to 127) \, the box beside it the value itself. \; PATCH band. name: the instrument that plays. library N: load instrument N of the whole library. reload: the instrument again \, edits dropped. init: all controls back to 0 \, the instrument stays. rescan: read the library folder again after adding files. \; folder: where you are \, as on the EPS: library is the top (disk images and EFE files) \, an image and its directories below (DISK1.img/BASSES). up: one folder up. file: the selected file with the arrows before and after it \, a directory ends in a slash. open: enter the directory or load the instrument. Loading an instrument shows its folder. \; Saving always writes the Ensoniq format \, so the EPS reads it. The offsets of the controls (cutoff \, attack ...) go into the instrument first and are set back to 0. replace: over the file the instrument was loaded from. save copy: a new file in the folder shown \, named after the instrument with a number. new name and save as: a new file of that name in the folder shown (in an image \, or an EFE at the top). new folder: a directory of that name \, inside an image. Names: up to 12 characters \, A-Z 0-9 space * - + (what the EPS lets you type) \, underscore for a space. See Library and saving. \; MIDI in: the receive channel \, 0 = all. out: the channel of the controller feedback. thru: see Messages \, thru. size: files in the folder \, library size: instruments in all. \; The other rows edit the loaded instrument itself (mouse only \, see Instrument rows). SELECT band: the layer and the wavesample the LAYER and WS rows show \, with their names and the key range \, root key \, rate and length of the wavesample. envelope: which one the ENV rows show (1 pitch \, 2 filter \, 3 amp). follow key (on by default): a played key selects its layer and wavesample \, the rows jump to their values. zero crossings (on by default): sample and loop points move to the nearest rising zero crossing. \; Sample and loop points have two cells each: the first sets the position coarsely \, in steps of 1/127 of the sample \, the second (fine) moves it by -64 to +63 samples from there. The box shows the sample number. Edits change the playing instrument at once (the next note \, filter \, amp \, pan \, LFO and wave modulation also sounding notes). They are kept with replace \, save copy or save as \, a reload or an instrument change drops them., f 64;
#X obj 60 1314 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Launchkey 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1342 Launchkey: the Novation Launchkey Mini MK4 with the custom firmware maltepeter2000/launchkey_firmware \, set up the same for every instrument of bela-workstation (launchkey/README.md there). \; epscc 1: the controller layer. Encoders in mode Custom 1 send CC pages from 20 in rows of 8 on the keys channel \, every change made elsewhere goes back to them on the send channel (txchannel of eps~) \, CC 3 asks for all values. \; epspads 10: the pads in Drum mode (notes 36-51 on channel 10 \, the Drums Channel): top row 36 factory + \, 37 user + \, 38 init (press twice within a second) \, 39-43 own \, bottom row 44 factory - \, 45 user - \, 46 save \, 47-51 own. Own pads of eps~: 39 patch select + \, 47 patch select -. \; Storing asks first: save once shows Store to? and the user slot played or stored last (savearm) \, user + / - choose the slot (saveslot 1 / -1 \, nothing is recalled) \, save again stores there and writes the user bank (save). Any other pad or 10 s cancel (savecancel). Own pads and init after its first press show what they do on the display. \; Pad colours \, sent by epspads itself with noteout on channel 10 (velocity = colour + 32 x mode): factory blue \, user and save green (the user bank) \, init orange (flashing while the second press is awaited) \, save flashing while it asks and white for a moment when stored \, own pads purple \, free pads dark. They go out at load \, on leds \, and when the Launchkey asks for them (CC 3 = 127 on channel 10 \, sent when Drum mode is selected). \; lkdisplay: the display lines (display <title> <value>: the control you turn \, Preset \, User \, Init or Stored with the patch name) as SysEx for the Launchkey's display -> midiout., f 64;
#X obj 60 1820 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Inlet 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1848 1 float: note number \, velocity 100. \; 1 list: note \, velocity and \, if you like \, the MIDI channel. \;   MIDI in eps.i~: notes \, bend \, pressure (channel and poly) \, \;   program change \, CC 1 wheel \, 2 XCTRL \, 4 pedal \, 7 volume \, \;   64 sustain. \; 1 anything: a message \, see Messages., f 64;
#X obj 60 1958 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Outlets 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1986 1 signal: left output. \; 2 signal: right output. \; 3 anything: info \, name \, 0-127 and value text for every \;   change \, the instrument that plays \, its name \, the channels. \; 3 loaded model layers wavesamples: an instrument is in. \; 3 display title value: two lines for a display: a control \;   (Cutoff \, -12) \, the instrument (Preset 3 \, CLASSIC_PAD) \, \;   Loading \, saving (Saved \, PAD_BRIGHT). \; 3 folder text n \, bsel k n kind name: the folder shown \, \;   its size and the selected file (kind dir or inst). \; 3 saved: an instrument was written., f 64;
#X obj 60 2176 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Library\ and\ saving 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2204 The library is the folder eps beside the patch: Ensoniq disk images (IMG from a floppy \, HDA or IMG images of the hard disk on the SD card of a ZuluSCSI or BlueSCSI \, EDE \, EDA \, GKH) and EFE files. The editor shows it as on the EPS: each image is a folder with its directories \, the EFE files lie at the top. preset N \, presetstep and program changes go through all instruments \, in the order of the file names and the directories. An instrument is read in the background: notes go on sounding until it is in. \; Saving keeps the Ensoniq format: into an image the file goes as the EPS writes it (checked against the original operating system: it lists and loads what eps~ saved) \, at the top level as an EFE. So your library can go back onto the SD card of the ZuluSCSI as it is. A directory holds 38 files \, names have 12 characters. \; Before eps~ writes into an image it checks it: the free block count must match the FAT and every directory its file count \, otherwise it refuses and changes nothing. Every block it changes is first copied to <image>.undo beside the image: build/eps_fs_tool undo <image> puts the image back as it was before the first change (and deletes the journal). Delete the .undo file once you are happy \, and keep a copy of your SD card anyway. \; There is one library in Pd \, not one per object: two eps.i~ in one patch share it. Save the patch first \, an unsaved patch has no folder of its own. \; The controls of eps~ are offsets over the instrument while you play. Saving writes transpose \, tune \, cutoff \, filter 2 \, filter env \, attack \, decay and release into the instrument (into every wavesample) and sets them back to 0. vibrato \, wave position \, bend range \, patch select and vibrato rate are playing controls \, the EPS has no place for them in an instrument: they stay. volume and voices belong to the instrument object., f 64;
#X obj 60 2746 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Messages 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2774 name v: set a control \, see Controls. \; name: report it as info. \; n name v: set it from a pot or a MIDI CC \, 0 to 127. \; ninfo name: report it as info. \; cycle name [-1]: the next (previous) value \, round and round. \; dump: every control as info. \; e name v: set an instrument parameter (see Instrument \;   rows) from 0 to 127. einfo name: report it as info. \; edump: the selection (sel) and every instrument parameter. \; sel layer N \, sel ws N \, sel env N (1-3): select what the \;   LAYER \, WS and ENV rows edit\; sel layerstep / wsstep +-1. \;   ws N counts within the layer (WS 1..n of it \, as on the EPS). \;   Answers sel <layer> <ws> <env> <layers> <layer name> \;   <ws name> <info> <wavesamples in the layer>. \; follow 0/1: a played key selects its layer and wavesample. \; zerox 0/1: points snap to zero crossings. \; library [dir]: use this folder as the library and read \;   its list. Without dir: eps beside the external. \; rescan: read the library folder again. \; preset n: load library instrument n (from 0) \, controls \;   back to 0. \; presetstep 1 or -1: the next or previous instrument. \; open file [k]: load instrument k (from 0) of any file \, \;   without the library. A name without a path is beside \;   the external. \; presets: list the library as preset n name. \; browse: report the folder shown (folder \, bsel). \; bstep 1 or -1 \, bselect k: move the selection in the folder. \; benter: enter the selected directory or load the selected \;   instrument. bup: the folder above. \; userstep 1 or -1: the next or previous instrument in the \;   folder of the one playing (the pads' user + and -). \; init: every control back to 0 \, the instrument stays. \; reload: the instrument again \, edits dropped. \; saveas name: write the instrument as a new file into the \;   folder shown: into the image \, or name.efe at the top. \;   Refused if the name is there. \; replace: write it over the file it was loaded from. \; save: saveas with a free name \, the instrument's and a \;   number (the pads' save). \; mkdir name: a new directory in the folder shown (images). \; SysEx with a real EPS-16 PLUS (MIDI both ways \, SYS-EX ON in \;   its EDIT / System-MIDI page): \; epsch n: the EPS's MIDI base channel \, 1-16 (default 1). \; fetch i: instrument i (1-8) from the EPS's memory into the \;   folder shown as a new file \, then loaded. The effect does \;   not come along (SysEx cannot carry it). \; fetchws i l w: wavesample w of layer l as name.wav into the \;   library folder. \; send i: the instrument that plays into the EPS's empty \;   instrument i (1-8\; delete it there first). Copies share \;   their data as in the file. The effect stays the EPS's. \; sendws i l w file.wav: the WAV (library folder) into \;   wavesample w of layer l \, which must exist. One that has \;   copies keeps its old length (the EPS will not cut it). \; sxcancel: stop a transfer. Outlet 3: sysex step percent \, \;   fetched \, sent. \;   All three write the offsets into the instrument first. \; bend f: pitch wheel \, -1 to 1 \, times bend. \; mod f: modulation wheel \, 0 to 1 (EPS source WHEEL). \; pressure f: channel pressure \, 0 to 1 (PRESS). \; polypressure note f: pressure of one key (PRESS). \; pedal f \, xctrl f: the sources PEDAL and XCTRL \, 0 to 1. \; sustain 0/1: hold pedal. \; channel n: MIDI receive channel \, 0 is all. \; txchannel n: channel of the controller feedback \, 1 to 16. \; thru 0/1: MIDI thru \, on by default. \; status: report instrument \, channels \, library size and \;   sounding voices. \; stop \, reset: all notes off., f 64;
#X obj 60 3908 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty The\ instrument 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3936 eps~ reads the instruments of the Ensoniq EPS \, EPS-16 PLUS and ASR-10 from their own files: layers \, wavesamples with their samples \, loops and loop modes \, envelopes \, filters \, pan \, patch select and the wave modulation (transwave). It plays them on a model of the ES5505 voice chip of the EPS-16 PLUS \, taken from MAME: interpolation \, the 4-pole filter in its four arrangements \, the 8-bit logarithmic volume \, forward \, backward and bidirectional loops \, the 2/1024 pitch steps. \; What the EPS operating system does is rebuilt on top: voice allocation \, layers and patch select \, the three envelopes (pitch \, filter \, amp) with the two-stage release \, attack velocity \, keyboard time scaling and the finite mode \, the LFO with its seven waves \, delay \, reset and humanize \, random pitch \, the modulation of pitch \, both filters \, amp and pan from the 15 sources (LFO \, RANDM \, ENV1 \, ENV2 \, PR+VL \, VEL \, VEL1 \, VEL2 \, KBD \, PITCH \, WHEEL \, PEDAL \, XCTRL \, PRESS \, WL+PR \, numbered as in the External Command Specification) \, crossfades \, layer glide and all seven wave modulation types (loop position \, sample start \, both \, loop start \, loop end \, loop start-X \, transwave). \; Calibrated against the original operating system (OS 1.30 in an emulator \, register traces of test instruments): cutoff scale of both filters \, volume and envelope level law \, pan law \, boost \, envelope times and shapes \, release \, LFO speeds and depth \, wave modulation types and range \, the pitch \, filter and key amounts \, the mod sources 0-11. Still approximations: the soft/hard velocity levels \, amp and pan modulation amounts \, crossfade curves \, glide (every mode is mono with glide). Not there: pitch tables and the effects of the ES5510. The controls of eps~ are offsets over the instrument \, 0 plays it as stored. \; The output runs at Pd's sample rate \, not at the 29.76 kHz of the EPS-16 PLUS. Up to 32 voices \, 20 by default., f 64;
#X obj 60 4462 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Data\ files 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 4490 Building needs no data. To play \, put Ensoniq instruments into the folder eps beside your patch: single files (.efe from EpsLin \, EnDiskEx or Chicken Translator) or whole disk images (.img from a floppy \, an SD card of a ZuluSCSI or BlueSCSI \, .ede and .eda from Giebler's tools \, .gkh). HFE floppy images are converted to IMG first. The EPS \, the EPS-16 PLUS and the ASR-10 share the format \, tested so far with EPS-16 PLUS instruments. \; For eps.i~ two files are enough: eps~ (the external) and eps.i~.pd in a folder on Pd's search path., f 64;
#X obj 60 4664 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Controls 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 4692 In the order of the editor: one heading per row \, the same title as the row there \, page k on the controller is row k. Each line: name (the label in the editor) \, range \, CC number and what it does. Page 1 follows the other instruments: bright \, resonance \, attack \, release \, vibrato \, effect (free for now) \, own (the wave position) and volume., f 64;
#X obj 60 4802 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 1\ \ PERFORM\ \ CC\ 20\ 21\ 22\ 23\ 24\ 26\ 27 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 4830 cut (Cutoff) -63..63 \, CC 20: cutoff of filter 1 of every \;   wavesample \, also of sounding notes. 0 leaves the instrument \;   as it is \; cut2 (Filter 2) -63..63 \, CC 21: cutoff of filter 2 (the second \;   low-pass \, or the high-pass in the modes with one) \, also of \;   sounding notes. The EPS has no resonance \, this takes its \;   place on page 1 \; att (Attack) -63..63 \, CC 22: attack: time 1 of the amp \;   envelope \, in steps of the panel's 0-99 times \, for the notes \;   that follow \; rel (Release) -63..63 \, CC 23: release: times 5 and 6 of the \;   amp envelope \, also for notes already held \; vib (Vibrato) 0..127 \, CC 24: vibrato of eps~ itself (not the \;   instrument's LFO) \, up to a semitone \; wave (Wave pos) 0..127 \, CC 26: position of the wave modulation \;   (transwave \, loop position): added to the wavesample's mod \;   source \, as if the wheel were turned up. No effect on \;   wavesamples without wave modulation \; volume (Volume) 0..127 \, CC 27: master volume \, 100 = unity \, not \;   stored, f 64;
#X obj 60 5164 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 2\ \ PLAY\ \ CC\ 28\ 29\ 30\ 31\ 33\ 34\ 35\ 36 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 5192 patch (Patch sel) 0..3 \, CC 28: patch select: which layers \;   play \, as the two patch buttons of the EPS \; transpose (Transpose) -24..24 \, CC 29: transposition in \;   semitones \, of the keyboard and the pitch \; tune (Tune) -50..50 \, CC 30: fine tuning in cents \; bend (Bend range) 0..12 \, CC 31: bender range in semitones \; dec (Decay) -63..63 \, CC 33: decay: times 2 to 4 of the amp \;   envelope \, for the notes that follow \; fenv (Filter env) -63..63 \, CC 34: added to the filter envelope \;   amount of every wavesample \, also of sounding notes \; vibrate (Vib rate) 0..127 \, CC 35: rate of the vibrato \, 0.1 to \;   12 Hz \; voices (Voices) 1..32 \, CC 36: polyphony: 20 as on the EPS-16 \;   PLUS \, up to the 32 of the ES5505 \, not stored, f 64;
#X obj 60 5430 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Instrument\ rows 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 5458 The rows below the controls edit the instrument (mouse only \, message e). Each line: name (label) and range of the value as stored \, what it is., f 64;
#X obj 60 5520 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty INSTRUMENT 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 5548 i_trans (Transpose) -36..36: transposition of the instrument \;   in semitones \; i_keylo (Key low) 21..108: lowest key the instrument plays \; i_keyhi (Key high) 21..108: highest key the instrument plays \; i_press (Pressure) 0..3: pressure mode as stored (0..3) \; i_psel (Patch mode) 0..3: patch select mode as stored \; i_prog (MIDI prog) 0..127: MIDI program as stored \; i_mout (MIDI out ch) 0..16: MIDI out channel as stored, f 64;
#X obj 60 5690 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty LAYER 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 5718 l_vello (Vel low) 0..127: lowest velocity the layer plays \; l_velhi (Vel high) 0..127: highest velocity the layer plays \; l_glmode (Glide mode) 0..5: glide mode as stored (eps~: any \;   mode is mono with glide) \; l_gltime (Glide time) 0..99: glide time \; l_delay (Delay) 0..99: delay before the layer sounds \; l_dlyvel (Delay vel) -127..127: velocity modulation of the \;   delay \; l_restrk (Restrike) 0..99: restrike decay \; l_legato (Legato) 0..1: legato layer, f 64;
#X obj 60 5892 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty LAYER\ PATCHES 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 5920 l_in00 (In 00) 0..1: the layer plays in patch 00 \; l_in0x (In 0*) 0..1: the layer plays in patch 0* \; l_inx0 (In *0) 0..1: the layer plays in patch *0 \; l_inxx (In **) 0..1: the layer plays in patch ** \; l_keyup (Key up) 0..1: the layer sounds on key up \; l_stlink (Stereo link) 0..1: stereo link \; l_ptable (Pitch table) 0..1: pitch table (not played by eps~), f 64;
#X obj 60 6046 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty WS\ POINTS 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 6074 w_sst (Smp start) 0..127: sample start \, coarse \; w_sstf (fine) 0..127: sample start \, fine \; w_send (Smp end) 0..127: sample end \, coarse \; w_sendf (fine) 0..127: sample end \, fine \; w_lst (Loop start) 0..127: loop start \, coarse \; w_lstf (fine) 0..127: loop start \, fine \; w_lend (Loop end) 0..127: loop end \, coarse \; w_lendf (fine) 0..127: loop end \, fine, f 64;
#X obj 60 6216 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty WS\ LOOP\ /\ WAVE\ MOD 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 6244 w_loop (Loop mode) 0..4: loop mode \; w_keylo (WS key low) 21..108: lowest key of the wavesample \; w_keyhi (WS key high) 21..108: highest key of the wavesample \; w_wmsrc (Wave mod) 0..15: source of the wave modulation \; w_wmamt (Wave amt) -127..127: amount of the wave modulation \; w_wmtype (Wave type) 0..7: type of the wave modulation \; w_wmrng (Wave range) 0..15: range of the wave modulation, f 64;
#X obj 60 6370 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty WS\ PITCH 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 6398 w_root (Root key) 21..108: root key \; w_fine (Fine) -127..127: fine tune \, 1/128 semitone \; w_bend (Bend range) 0..13: bender range in semitones \, 13 = \;   global \; w_penv (ENV1 pitch) -127..127: pitch envelope amount \; w_plfo (LFO pitch) -127..127: LFO to pitch \; w_psrc (Pitch mod) 0..15: pitch modulation source \; w_pamt (Pitch amt) -127..127: pitch modulation amount \; w_rand (Random) 0..127: random pitch amount, f 64;
#X obj 60 6556 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty WS\ FILTER\ 1 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 6584 w_fmode (Filt mode) 0..3: filter mode (0 \, 1: filter 2 is a \;   high-pass) \; w_cut1 (Cutoff 1) 0..150: cutoff of filter 1 \; w_key1 (Key 1) -127..127: key tracking of filter 1 \; w_env1 (Env 1) -127..127: ENV2 amount on filter 1 \; w_fsrc1 (Mod 1) 0..15: modulation source of filter 1 \; w_famt1 (Mod amt 1) -127..127: modulation amount of filter 1, f 64;
#X obj 60 6710 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty WS\ FILTER\ 2 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 6738 w_cut2 (Cutoff 2) 0..150: cutoff of filter 2 \; w_key2 (Key 2) -127..127: key tracking of filter 2 \; w_env2 (Env 2) -127..127: ENV2 amount on filter 2 \; w_fsrc2 (Mod 2) 0..15: modulation source of filter 2 \; w_famt2 (Mod amt 2) -127..127: modulation amount of filter 2, f 64;
#X obj 60 6832 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty WS\ AMP 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 6860 w_vol (WS volume) 0..127: volume of the wavesample \; w_pan (Pan) -128..127: pan (EPS-16 PLUS / ASR) \; w_boost (Boost) 0..1: +12 dB \; w_asrc (Amp mod) 0..15: amp modulation source \; w_aamt (Amp amt) -127..127: amp modulation amount \; w_pnsrc (Pan mod) 0..15: pan modulation source \; w_pnamt (Pan amt) -127..127: pan modulation amount \; w_bus (Out bus) 0..7: output bus as stored, f 64;
#X obj 60 7002 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty WS\ LFO 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 7030 w_lwave (LFO wave) 0..6: LFO waveform \; w_lspeed (LFO speed) 0..99: LFO speed \; w_ldepth (LFO depth) 0..127: LFO depth \; w_ldelay (LFO delay) 0..99: LFO delay \; w_lmode (LFO mode) 0..2: LFO restart mode \; w_lrsrc (Rate mod) 0..15: LFO rate modulation source \; w_lramt (Rate amt) -127..127: LFO rate modulation amount \; w_rfreq (Random freq) 0..99: rate of the random pitch, f 64;
#X obj 60 7172 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty WS\ XFADE\ /\ LFO\ DEPTH 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 7200 w_xfa (Xfade A) 0..127: crossfade point A \; w_xfb (Xfade B) 0..127: crossfade point B \; w_xfc (Xfade C) 0..127: crossfade point C \; w_xfd (Xfade D) 0..127: crossfade point D \; w_xfcrv (Xfade on) 0..1: amp modulation as crossfade \; w_ldsrc (Depth mod) 0..15: LFO depth modulation source \; w_ldamt (Depth amt) -127..127: LFO depth modulation amount, f 64;
#X obj 60 7326 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty ENV\ LEVELS\ HARD 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 7354 e_l0h (L0 hard) 0..127: start level \, hard \; e_l1h (L1 hard) 0..127: level 1 \, hard \; e_l2h (L2 hard) 0..127: level 2 \, hard \; e_l3h (L3 hard) 0..127: level 3 \, hard \; e_l4h (L4 hard) 0..127: sustain level \, hard \; e_l5 (L5 release) -127..127: level change of the first release \;   stage \; e_mode (Env mode) 0..2: envelope mode \; e_type (Soft curve) 0..4: soft velocity curve as stored, f 64;
#X obj 60 7512 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty ENV\ LEVELS\ SOFT 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
